\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [{e}^{14}+10,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge {e}^{14}+10.$" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317962 13194139534893 true] [17592186317961 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534893 true] [17592186317961 1041 "field-1" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317961 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317962 13194139534893 true] [17592186317961 1110 #uuid "3dac418b-8090-533a-9b21-0d06af629ce3" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317962 1034 "\\ge" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317962 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534893 true] [17592186317962 1111 #uuid "f5eea139-b136-5137-9fab-3b6ae9daa7f5" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317963 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317964 13194139534893 true] [17592186317963 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534893 true] [17592186317963 1041 "field-2" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317963 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317964 13194139534893 true] [17592186317963 1110 #uuid "efce137b-7783-5586-bc62-1c3223128b09" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317964 1034 "10+e^{14}" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534893 true] [17592186317964 1111 #uuid "e452deae-529e-5f25-ac44-d802af087cb1" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317965 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317966 13194139534893 true] [17592186317965 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317968 13194139534893 true] [17592186317965 1089 "q-278753" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317965 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534893 true] [17592186317965 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317965 1113 #uuid "a45f16c4-78c7-510b-9bcb-5c1470f6997a" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317965 1145 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)}\\cdot {e}^{-1} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ (2x+4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{e} & <x-2 \\\\ 2x+4 & <xe-2e \\\\ 4+2e & <xe-2x \\\\ 4+2e & <x(e-2) \\\\ x & >\\frac{4+2e}{e-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-2 \\\\ x-2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-2$ and $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty )$ and $(2,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty )\\cap (-2,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\nx>\\frac{4+2e}{e-2}.\n$$" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317966 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317967 13194139534893 true] [17592186317966 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534893 true] [17592186317966 1041 "field-1" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317966 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317967 13194139534893 true] [17592186317966 1110 #uuid "cf333ee9-41b7-51a6-b8f4-237322a0a13d" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317967 1034 ">" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317967 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534893 true] [17592186317967 1111 #uuid "3f90360a-6b04-56ac-ba8f-f870ce198097" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317969 13194139534893 true] [17592186317968 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534893 true] [17592186317968 1041 "field-2" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317968 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317969 13194139534893 true] [17592186317968 1110 #uuid "9eda7b92-309b-5a67-9183-210c1ce5c29c" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317969 1034 "\\frac{2e+4}{e-2}" 13194139534893 true] [17592186317969 1103 #edb/ref 1759